{"kind":"request","text":"codex: spike two of three from the JSONata/DDL implementation note — the query sandbox. Hugh has authorised progressing all three spikes and then the chess replatform; this is the first because its failure is the one that would change the design rather than refine it.\n\nWHY THIS ONE FIRST, and I am choosing the order rather than filing three at once. The note's own stop rule says failure of the historical-view or query-sandbox spike is grounds to change the driver or narrow the SQL surface. Of those two, the sandbox is where a failure is both most likely and most expensive to discover late: it is the boundary between an agent-authored query and a durable log, and if the authorizer cannot actually hold that line then the driver choice and the SQL surface are both wrong, and spikes one and three would be built on it. Cheapest thing that can invalidate the design goes first.\n\nIt also answers a question already on the record. The external reviewer raised agent-generated SQL running over attacker-authored rows; I assessed that the fold refuses the dangerous durable acts, and said the boundary here is a SQLite authorizer rather than the capacity bounds. That assessment is exactly what this spike tests. If the authorizer does not hold, my answer to the reviewer was wrong and I would rather find out from a spike than from a reader.\n\nWHAT THE NOTE ASKS FOR, quoted rather than paraphrased so nothing is softened: prove the application-query connection refuses every write, DDL, PRAGMA, attachment, extension, and unsafe function; cancels expensive queries; bounds results; and permits a reader while the fold transaction is open.\n\nWHAT I WANT BEYOND A GREEN RESULT. A spike that passes tells us less than a spike that says what it could not close. For each of the six refusal classes, say whether it is refused by the authorizer, by a runtime limit, by convention, or not at all — and if any is held by convention rather than by the engine, name it plainly, because that is the one a future change will quietly break. The concurrent-reader case is the one most likely to disappoint: if a reader cannot in fact proceed while the fold transaction is open, say so and say what that costs, rather than adjusting the claim to match the result.\n\nThe note's own boundary applies and I am restating it as a condition: failure here is grounds to change the driver or narrow the SQL surface, and is not grounds to add database concepts to the sequencing kernel or to enlarge the minimal application. If you find yourself wanting to move something into the kernel to make this pass, stop and file that as a finding instead.\n\nSCOPE. This is a spike under spike/, not productionisation — codex's own closure report bd203d4c put productionisation explicitly out of scope and that has not changed. Sequenced after your current lanes: the chess seat-threshold repair I have just filed at 5865cd70 comes first, since chess has been blocked on me and is unblocked now.","body":{"conditions":"Run the query-sandbox spike from notes/2026-08-26-jsonata-ddl-application-implementation.md under spike/, without productionising anything. Prove the application-query connection refuses every write, DDL, PRAGMA, attachment, extension and unsafe function; cancels expensive queries; bounds results; and permits a reader while the fold transaction is open. For each refusal class state which mechanism holds it — SQLite authorizer, runtime limit, convention, or nothing — and name plainly any class held by convention rather than by the engine. Tests must be mutation-sensitive: for at least the authorizer and the cancellation path, disable the guard, watch the named assertion fail, restore, and confirm the tree is clean. Report what the spike could not close as well as what it proved; a class you could not test is a finding, not an omission. If the concurrent-reader case does not hold, say so and say what it costs rather than narrowing the claim to fit the result. The note's boundary is a condition here: failure is grounds to change the driver or narrow the SQL surface, and is not grounds to add database concepts to the sequencing kernel or to enlarge the minimal application; if you want to move something into the kernel to make this pass, stop and file that as a finding. Exact clean pushed head, gofmt, build, vet, git diff --check and go test ./... -count=1 green, per-path artifacts at every changed stable path resting on your promise plus stable bases, and independent review recording Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions. Do not merge. Sequenced after the chess seat-threshold repair 5865cd70.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}